WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
Let us summarize the major ideas from Module 6.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:09.387
Start with grain. Decide what one result row should represent before writing the join.

00:00:09.387 --> 00:00:14.377
Then think about preservation. INNER JOIN keeps only matches.

00:00:14.377 --> 00:00:18.392
LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN preserve one designated side.

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:22.572
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides.

00:00:22.572 --> 00:00:27.972
Remember that ON controls matching while WHERE filters the joined result.

00:00:27.972 --> 00:00:31.597
This difference is especially important with outer joins.

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:35.627
Cardinality explains why rows repeat or multiply.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:42.292
One-to-many repetition can be perfectly correct, but several one-to-many child tables joined together can

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:45.447
create combinations that inflate counts.

00:00:45.447 --> 00:00:50.007
When your target is one row per patient, customer, student, or department,

00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:54.072
pre-aggregating child tables is often the clearest solution.

00:00:54.072 --> 00:01:01.867
Finally, validate. Compare total rows, distinct keys, unmatched cases, and known examples.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:08.502
If you keep one habit from this module, let it be this: define the grain, predict the matches, run the join,

00:01:08.502 --> 00:01:12.217
and verify the result before trusting it.

00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:15.553
That workflow will help you far beyond this course.
